    "section_summary": {
      "type": "string",
      "extract_value": "This section describes the Self-Compassion Scale (SCS; Neff, 2003; Costa et al., 2015), a 26-item self-report measure with positive (self-kindness, common humanity, mindfulness) and negative (self-judgment, isolation, over-identification) components. The authors created a composite (SCS_SC) from the three positive subscales for the present study; the SCS_SC demonstrated Cronbach's alpha = 0.91. The section does not mention Study 1, sample size, country, participant demographics, BMI assessment, body appreciation assessment, survey method, or number of studies.",
      "evidence_source": "\"The SCS evaluates self-compassion through a positive and a negative component.\" \"The 26-item self-report scale is rated in a 5-point scale, which ranges from 0 (\\\"Almost never\\\") to 5 (\\\"Almost always\\\").\" \"Taking into account the aims of the present study, and empirical support by Phillipps and Ferguson (2012) and by Costa et al. (2015), the three positive subscales were gathered into a composite measure defined as self-compassion (SCS_SC), while the negative subscales were not used.\" \"The SCS_SC revealed a Cronbach's alpha value of 0.91.\"",
